#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148455} :activity/id #uuid "792dc37c-94db-5522-a998-3f524ef92eee" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048605 :topic/id #uuid "adefe8a5-2a87-5789-91e4-05399a499f4a" :topic/math-academy-id 2531} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148455 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148756 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we will solve first-order *initial value problems* using Laplace transforms.\n\nThe key idea is that the Laplace transform converts derivatives into algebraic expressions involving the transform of the unknown function.\nIf we write\n\n$$\nY(s) = \\mathcal L\\{y(t)\\},\n$$\n\nthen the initial condition appears automatically through the **derivative rule**\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{y'\\} = sY(s) - y(0).\n$$\n\nSo, when we take the Laplace transform of a differential equation like\n\n$$\ny' + ay = f(t),\n$$\n\nwe get an algebraic equation in $Y(s),$ which we can solve using standard algebra.\n\nIn the first few problems, our goal will be to find an explicit formula for $Y(s).$\nLater, we will finish the process by taking the inverse Laplace transform to recover $y(t).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "19b12099-79ea-57ad-8d49-b0ec631e099d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4111 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "ce168e7b-e179-5ef7-a408-d6641bda4d96" :step/math-academy-id 612632 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148456}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148456 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148667 :tutorial/content "The **Laplace transform method** is a powerful tool that converts a differential equation (a calculus problem) into an algebraic equation, which is often much easier to solve.\n\nTo solve a first-order initial value problem (IVP) of the form\n\n$$\ny' + ay = f(t), \\quad y(0)=y_0,\n$$\n\nwe follow a standard workflow.\n\n- First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides of the equation to obtain an algebraic equation in $Y(s)=\\mathcal L\\{y(t)\\}.$ Using linearity and the derivative formula\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{y'\\} = sY(s) - y(0),\n$$\n\nwe convert the IVP into an algebraic equation for the unknown transform, $Y(s).$\n\n- After substituting the initial condition $y(0)=y_0$ and finding the transform of $f(t),$ we solve the resulting algebraic equation for $Y(s).$ The calculus portion of the problem is now complete.\n\n- Before applying the inverse transform, we rewrite $Y(s)$ as a sum of simpler expressions found in a standard table of Laplace transforms. This step often requires techniques like **partial fraction decomposition**.\n\n- Finally, we take the inverse Laplace transform, $\\mathcal L^{-1},$ of each term in $Y(s)$ to find the solution $y(t)$ to the original initial value problem.\n\nNext, we consider the example of finding $Y(s)$ for the concrete initial value problem." :tutorial/id #uuid "2a58657b-748f-56e6-9ebc-7cb481b9bf57" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14889 :tutorial/title "Using Laplace Transforms to Solve First-Order IVPs"} :step/id #uuid "fa47c1d6-1923-5ff4-aafc-fbb54002fb57" :step/math-academy-id 612633 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148457}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148457 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148668 :tutorial/content "Consider the following initial value problem.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t} + 4y = 2t-1, \\qquad y(0)=0.\n$$\n\nTo find $Y(s) = \\mathcal L \\{y(t)\\},$ we first take the Laplace transform of both sides of the differential equation. Using the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{y'\\} + 4\\mathcal{L}\\{y\\} = 2\\mathcal{L}\\{t\\}-\\mathcal{L}\\{1\\}.\n$$\n\nTo calculate $\\mathcal L\\left\\{ y' \\right\\},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{y'\\right\\}  = sY(s) - y(0)   = sY(s).\n$$\n\nSo, the differential equation transforms to\n\n$$\nsY(s) + 4Y(s)   = 2\\mathcal{L}\\{t\\}-\\mathcal{L}\\{1\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, we make use of the following result:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{t^n\\} = \\dfrac{n!}{s^{n+1}}, \\qquad s > 0, \\quad n\\in \\mathbb{N}_0.\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{t} & =\\frac{1!}{{s}^{1+1}}=\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}, \\\\ L{1} & =\\frac{0!}{{s}^{0+1}}=\\frac{1}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these results into the transformed equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sY(s)+4Y(s) & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{s} \\\\ (s+4)Y(s) & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{s} \\\\ (s+4)Y(s) & =\\frac{2-s}{{s}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, dividing by $(s+4),$ we obtain\n\n$$\nY(s) =\\dfrac{2-s}{s^2(s + 4)}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "fc740a47-119f-5fa2-bac2-018828c79406" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14890 :tutorial/title "Finding the Laplace Transform of the Unknown Function Satisfying the IVP"} :step/id #uuid "8da8db44-ae26-54f5-ba7f-f8256253813f" :step/math-academy-id 612634 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148458}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148458 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147527 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147362 :question/id #uuid "700f5e8f-33f6-568a-ad11-d2664336725d" :question/math-academy-id "e-6657"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1417a42-f47a-5611-84be-f331c75287a9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048580} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049301} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050608}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148153} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317269} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317357} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335707} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335708}] :knowledge-point/title "Laplace Transforms of First-Order Linear IVPs"} :step/id #uuid "451b2242-11ee-5088-a876-11e2c54ac097" :step/math-academy-id 612635 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148459}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148459 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148665 :tutorial/content "Once we have solved for $Y(s)$, the final step is to compute the solution .\n\nThe main idea is to rewrite $Y(s)$ as a sum of simpler terms whose inverse transforms are known from a table.\n\n**Step 1:** Look for standard inverse-transform patterns.\n\nThe goal is to match terms in $Y(s)$ to known transform pairs. Common forms include:\n\n- $\\mathcal{L}^{-1}\\left\\{\\dfrac{1}{s-a}\\right\\} = e^{at}$\n\n- $\\mathcal{L}^{-1}\\left\\{\\dfrac{1}{(s-a)^n}\\right\\} = \\dfrac{t^{n-1}e^{at}}{(n-1)!}$ for integer $n \\ge 1$\n\n- $\\mathcal{L}^{-1}\\left\\{\\dfrac{s}{s^2+\\omega^2}\\right\\} = \\cos(\\omega t)$\n\n- $\\mathcal{L}^{-1}\\left\\{\\dfrac{\\omega}{s^2+\\omega^2}\\right\\} = \\sin(\\omega t)$\n\n**Step 2:** If $Y(s)$ is a rational function, use **partial fraction decomposition**.\n\nIf the denominator has distinct linear factors, we write:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{P(s)}{(s-a)(s-b)} = \\dfrac{A}{s-a} + \\dfrac{B}{s-b}\n$$\n\nwhere $P(s)$ is a polynomial with $\\deg (P) < 2$.\n\nIf there is a repeated linear factor, we include a term for each power:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{P(s)}{(s-a)^2} = \\dfrac{A}{s-a} + \\dfrac{B}{(s-a)^2}\n$$\n\nwhere $P(s)$ is a polynomial with $\\deg (P) < 2$.\n\n**Step 3:** Apply the inverse transform term-by-term.\n\nBecause the inverse Laplace transform is **linear**, we can apply it to each term in the sum separately to find the final solution $y(t)$.\n\nNext, we will apply this method to a specific example." :tutorial/id #uuid "785dff4c-cd6a-5cdd-bbcf-7c6b0c82c077" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14742 :tutorial/title "Getting Ready for the Inverse Laplace Transform"} :step/id #uuid "d4f76515-92a2-55e5-b0c3-2c8b3edd59d2" :step/math-academy-id 612638 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148460}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148460 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147528 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147363 :question/id #uuid "409c5aa3-5c53-5ab4-b2f7-bcfe01a14914" :question/math-academy-id "e-6661"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5eae6b5d-adff-53b2-970d-f0dcee58365f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050050} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050768} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050964}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147787} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147788} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317272} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317338} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317340} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391797} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391831}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Initial Value Problems Using Laplace Transforms"} :step/id #uuid "71fe0cf6-2b8b-5473-b14f-c3ddf9ebb1d5" :step/math-academy-id 612639 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148461}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148461 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148666 :tutorial/content "When $Y(s)$ is a rational function, we often use partial fractions to rewrite it in a form where we can apply $\\mathcal L^{-1}.$\n\nSometimes the denominator contains an *irreducible quadratic factor*, such as $s^2+\\omega^2.$\nIn that case, the partial fraction term must have a linear numerator.\n\n**Step 1:** Write the correct decomposition form.\n\nFor example, if\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\dfrac{P(s)}{(s-a)(s^2+\\omega^2)},\n$$\n\nthen we decompose it as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{P(s)}{(s-a)(s^2+\\omega^2)}\n= \\dfrac{A}{s-a} + \\dfrac{Bs+C}{s^2+\\omega^2}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Multiply both sides by the denominator.\n\nClearing denominators gives an identity in $s{:}$\n\n$$\nP(s)=A(s^2+\\omega^2) + (Bs+C)(s-a).\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Solve for the constants.\n\nWe can find $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ by expanding the right-hand side and matching coefficients of powers of $s.$\n\n**Step 4:** Take the inverse Laplace transform.\n\nWe split the quadratic term to match the standard forms:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{Bs+C}{s^2+\\omega^2} = B\\left(\\dfrac{s}{s^2+\\omega^2}\\right) + \\dfrac{C}{\\omega}\\left(\\dfrac{\\omega}{s^2+\\omega^2}\\right).\n$$\n\nThen we apply the inverse transforms:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L^{-1}\\left\\{\\dfrac{s}{s^2+\\omega^2}\\right\\}=\\cos(\\omega t),\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal L^{-1}\\left\\{\\dfrac{\\omega}{s^2+\\omega^2}\\right\\}=\\sin(\\omega t).\n$$\n\nLet's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "0db35afe-1ae8-50e9-a89d-2c65a3bc7956" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14743 :tutorial/title "Partial Fractions With Irreducible Quadratic Factors"} :step/id #uuid "7fe41923-9e55-52ed-b19d-70a8608c1ef8" :step/math-academy-id 612643 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148462}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148462 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147529 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147233 :question/id #uuid "abd7efc4-f397-564d-a471-17b1ffc93fb3" :question/math-academy-id "e-21862"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "06fedec8-5aed-52f9-bd4f-aa81b9180ad4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050213}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147790} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317344} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317345} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317351} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385259} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397600}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Initial Value Problems With Sinusoidal Forcing"} :step/id #uuid "650337b0-ab16-5d31-b033-88945afb53bf" :step/math-academy-id 612644}] :activity/title "Solving First-Order ODEs Using Laplace Transforms" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147710}]}
